@scoped(['app/Filament/**', 'app/Livewire/**', 'app/Policies/**', 'app/Providers/Filament/**', 'resources/views/filament/**', 'resources/views/livewire/**'])
## Filament 5: authorization

- Filament 5 opens resource pages and renders their create, edit, view and delete buttons according to the resource's `get*AuthorizationResponse()` methods, which ask the model policy. Overriding only `can*()` leaves buttons on screen that end in a 403, and modal create actions skip the check entirely. With neither a policy nor overridden responses, everything is allowed. Give every resource one or the other (recipe and per-role test: `filament-panel-authorization` skill).
- Filament authorizes only its built-in CRUD operations. Authorize everything else yourself: custom pages (`canAccess()`), widgets (`canView()`), relation managers (`canViewForRecord()`), custom actions (`->authorize()` or `->visible()`) and custom Livewire components. A custom page inside a resource checks only the resource's `viewAny`; give it its own `canAccess()` when it shows or does more than the list.
- A cluster's own address (`/settings`) is open to everyone: `Cluster` keeps `Page::canAccess()`, which returns true, so whoever may open none of its pages lands on an empty page there — the sidebar entry is hidden (`shouldRegisterNavigation()` checks the pages), a typed or stale address is not. Override `canAccess()` on your clusters with `canAccessClusteredComponents()`. The panel lists its clusters among `getPages()` as well: code that walks the pages (a navigation guard, a test instantiating every page) skips `Cluster` subclasses.
- A resource's default slug mirrors its namespace below `Resources`: `Resources\Tasks\TaskDefinitionResource` answers at `tasks/task-definitions`, `Resources\Documents\DocumentResource` at `documents` (the directory is the model's plural). Moving a resource into a sub-namespace or a cluster changes its address, so set `$slug` on resources whose addresses people see or tests type.
- An action that a custom Blade view prints itself (the page's action property echoed, or the action invoked with its arguments) is rendered whatever `visible()` and `hidden()` say: `toHtml()` checks no visibility, so a hidden action shows as a disabled button instead of being left out. Print it only when `isVisible()` returns true, asked of the invoked action (invoking clones it with its arguments), since visibility that depends on the arguments is only right there. `assertActionHidden()` passes either way; only the rendered page shows the button.
- Bulk actions check `deleteAny()`-style abilities, not `delete()` per record. Use `->authorizeIndividualRecords()` when record rules matter, or deny the bulk ability.
- A table action that links to a resource page (edit, view) never runs its own `->using()` or `->action()` closure. Put guards (record rules, step-up confirmation, who may act) in the page (`handleRecordUpdate()`, `handleRecordCreation()`) or in the domain service it calls.
- Resource queries return every row. Restrict what a user may see in the resource's `getEloquentQuery()`, not by hiding buttons.
- Livewire restores properties and runs `boot()` and per-property hydrate hooks before Filament authorizes the request. Don't write data, dispatch events or call services from those hooks.
@endscoped

@scoped(['app/Filament/**', 'app/Enums/**', 'app/Providers/**', 'lang/**', 'resources/views/**'])
## Filament 5: forms and output

- A closure may receive an enum-backed field's state as the enum case or as its backing value, depending on the field and the model's casts. Compare through a helper that accepts both; a bare comparison with the case's `value` silently never matches the enum.
- RichEditor HTML is not sanitized on the server. Never print it unescaped in a Blade view: sanitize at render time with `Str::sanitizeHtml()` (Filament's macro over symfony/html-sanitizer). Table columns and infolist entries using `->html()` already sanitize.
- `RichContentRenderer::toHtml()` sanitizes everything it outputs, the HTML a custom block's `toHtml()` returns included, and the sanitizer keeps only HTML's safe elements and attributes (plus `class`, `style`, `data-id` and `data-type`): a block's inline SVG, such as a chart or a diagram, does not survive. Let such blocks return a marker, swap their own markup in after sanitizing (escaping every value in it yourself), and hand the result to an infolist entry as an `Htmlable` state, which it prints as it is; `->html()` on a string state would sanitize it again.
- A custom block's settings are stored in the editor HTML as JSON in its `data-config` attribute, written by PHP's `json_encode()` (a slash as `\/`, non-ASCII characters as `\u` escapes). A string replacement over stored editor HTML (placeholders, file paths) misses what sits in a block's settings, or breaks their JSON when the new text holds a quote: decode the attribute, rewrite its values and encode it again.
- An action opens its modal as soon as it has a custom modal heading, a modal description or modal content, with or without a schema, and a click no longer runs it at once. `callAction()` mounts and submits in one go, so a feature test can't tell; only the browser shows the extra window. Set these only when there is something to show, or hide the modal with `modalHidden()` for the cases that need none.
- Enums shown in tables, filters, badges or selects implement `HasLabel` (and `HasColor` where useful) with translated labels. Raw enum values never reach the UI.
- `preventFilePathTampering()` accepts a file already in the field only when the record's original value of the attribute named like the field holds its path as a string. Without a record (a schema in a plain Livewire component), inside a JSON column, or over a column that stores more than paths, every existing file is refused as tampered unless `allowFilePathUsing:` decides; let that closure accept only the files the record owns.
- Since Filament 5.10.3 the upload field shows a stored file under the name `getUploadedFileUsing()` returns; up to 5.10.2 it showed a stored image, audio or video file (while previews were on), and any file whose type it didn't know, under the last segment of its URL path instead. For files stored under generated names, return the original name there (`storeFileNamesIn()` keeps it) and put it into the file's URL as well, so a download or an opened file carries it too; on read-only pages, list the files in your own markup (thumbnails, download links).
- A single-file upload field replaces its file in place when another is added (FilePond's default): it removes the old upload on the server, deleting its temporary file, while the new upload is still on its way, and the new upload is lost — the browser keeps a reference to the deleted file, the server an empty field. Where users replace a file before submitting (a wizard that sends them back to fix an uploaded file), use `multiple()->maxFiles(1)`: a second file then waits until the first is removed, and nothing is replaced in place.
- The upload field stores a file under a ULID unless told otherwise, and a ULID encodes the moment it was made; an image also keeps its camera metadata (device, place, time taken) unless the server re-encodes it. Where an upload must not reveal who sent it or when, such as an anonymous submission, store it through `saveUploadedFileUsing()` under a random name and re-encode images on the server.
- `fill()` gives a field its `default()` only while the field is visible. A field that appears later, after a choice the form starts without, keeps a null state, so a required field with a default fails validation. Put the values such fields start with into the array passed to `fill()` in `mount()`.
- Filament calls a field's `afterStateUpdated()` only for an update that names the field's state path. Livewire 4 can send the whole parent property instead (`data` with every field), seen when several fields changed while another request was in flight. Code that saves field by field from `afterStateUpdated()` also handles the whole update in the component's hook for that property (`updatedData($value, $key)` with `$key` null), saving what differs from the stored state; otherwise those values are silently never saved.
- Filament's default avatar provider points every user's avatar at ui-avatars.com, so every browser sends that outside service its address, the page it is on and the user's initials, on every page. Where the app must not contact third parties, register a local provider with `defaultAvatarProvider()`; an inline SVG of the initials needs no request.
- The rich editor saves an emptied field as `<p></p>`, not null. Normalize markup without text or media to null before storing or comparing, or an emptied field still counts as filled.
- A notification's `title()` and `body()` take a string (or a closure returning one), not an `Htmlable`, and Filament prints both as sanitized HTML. Pass markup as a plain string (`<br>` between lines), and escape plain text from users with `e()` first, or a `<` in it is read as a tag.
- The default dashboard shows every widget the panel registers, including everything `discoverWidgets()` finds. Keep a widget meant for one page outside the discovered directory (for example in its resource's `Widgets` namespace), or it shows up on the dashboard too.
- A widget printed in a page's own view with the `livewire` directive is never lazy: Filament makes widgets lazy only where it renders them itself (dashboards, a page's header and footer widgets), by merging `'lazy' => true` from `Widget::getDefaultProperties()` into their parameters. Printed directly, each widget mounts and renders inside the page's request, so several widgets that each compute the same report (a chart per figure) compute it once per widget there, and the page waits for all of them. Compute it once in the page and hand it to each widget as a `mount()` argument (declare it there: Livewire 4 renders a parameter that is neither a public property nor a `mount()` argument as an HTML attribute of the component's root), taken only when it was computed for the widget's own parameters, and for the same user where what it shows depends on who looks. Such a widget must stay inline: a lazy one carries its mount parameters through the page in a snapshot, where an object Livewire cannot serialize fails and one it can is sent to the browser.
- Filament renders the global search field, key bindings included, only while the panel has a search provider and at least one of its resources returns true from `canGloballySearch()`, which by default needs a record title attribute and access to the resource. A custom `GlobalSearchProvider` that searches beyond resources shows no field until then: let one resource's `canGloballySearch()` return whether the user may search (only with a custom provider; the default one would then list that resource's records).
- For a German (or other noun-capitalizing) UI: Filament lower-cases a label's first letter in validation messages. Keep labels as written by setting `validationAttribute()` in `Field::configureUsing()`. Publish and translate Laravel's `validation`, `auth`, `passwords` and `pagination` lang files, or errors and error pages show in English.
- Filament renders the `PanelsRenderHook::FOOTER` hook in both of its layouts: under every page of the panel and under the simple pages (sign-in, password reset). Register content meant for all of them, such as links to legal pages, for `FOOTER` alone; registered for `SIMPLE_PAGE_END` as well, it shows twice on the sign-in pages.
- A table whose rows open by an action rather than a URL (an edit action in a modal on a `ManageRecords` page, a relation manager) wraps each cell's content in a `<button>`. A column whose state holds a button of its own, such as an Alpine "show more" toggle, then breaks: the browser's parser closes the outer button at the inner `<button>` tag and moves it out of its `x-data` scope, and Alpine resolves the scope's names globally — `open` becomes `window.open`, which it calls — so every row logs "Uncaught TypeError: Illegal invocation". Give such a column `disabledClick()`, which renders its cell unwrapped. Rows that open by URL are wrapped in a link and not affected.
@endscoped

@scoped(['app/Filament/**', 'app/Providers/**', 'resources/css/**', 'resources/views/**'])
## Filament 5: themes and phones

- Filament's own styles sit in cascade layers (`base`, `components`, `utilities`). A custom theme's rules outside any layer override them whatever their specificity, Tailwind's utility classes included: keep such rules narrow (a breakpoint, a class of your own), or they restyle every panel at every width.
- `stackedOnMobile()` puts each cell's column label into the markup, hidden from `sm` up, so a cell's `textContent` starts with the label: read `.fi-ta-cell-content`. Every cell also wraps its content in a link of its own to the record, so a stacked row is several small targets. To make the row one tap target, stretch the first cell's link over the row with an absolutely positioned `::after` (stacked rows are positioned below `sm`) and keep the row's other controls positioned above it.
- Filament's link pins its label to the baseline (`.fi-link-label`). In a link made taller, such as for a 44 px touch target, the label rides above the link's icon: centre the label too.
@endscoped
@endif
